Welcome!

This file gives an overview of the Term49 shell, and is
broken down into topics:

1. QUICK START
2. PREFERENCES
3. WHAT'S INSTALLED
4. FOR DEVELOPERS

===========================================================

1. QUICK START

You can access the various special keys (Crtl, Alt, arrows,
etc.) by touching the top left corner of the screen, and
then pressing special keys on the keyboard. When you touch
the top left corner of the screen, you will enter
'metamode', which remaps the keyboard keys to other
functions. You can tell when you are in metamode by the
green 'M' in the top right corner of the screen. Touching
the top left corner of the screen will toggle metamode on
and off. The default metamode keys are:

  h: left arrow
  j: down arrow
  k: up arrow
  l: right arrow
  t: tab
  e: escape
  c: control
  a: alt
  v: paste
  s: rescreen
  i: font size increase
  o: font size decrease
  p: font size reset
  r: reload ~/.term49.lua

After pressing a key in metamode you will be returned to
normal input, excepting the arrow keys, which leave you in
metamode so you can move the cursor around easily.

For example, if you wanted to type Ctrl-C (^C), then you
would touch the top left corner of the screen to enter
metamode, press 'c' to hold down control, and then 'c'
again to send ^C. If you wanted to move the cursor left,
you would touch the top left corner of the screen to enter
metamode, then 'h' to move the cursor left (perhaps several
times). Once you have the cursor where you want it, you can
start typing again by touching the top left corner of the
screen (exiting metamode). Typing any key that is not bound
to a metamode function will simply exit metamode.

If you have a physical keyboard on your device, you can hold
the space key to toggle metamode on, and also hold down
ordinary keys to get uppercase letters.

If your device has a physical shift key, or you are using a
bluetooth keyboard, you can also doubletap the right shift
key to toggle metamode on and off. Note that some keyboards
may not send right shift when you press the right shift key
(it may send left shift), but this can be addressed in
preferences.

The metamode keys are all configurable, as described below.

===========================================================

2. PREFERENCES

Preferences are read from the .term49.lua file in $HOME.
If this file does not exist at startup it will be created
at launch time with the default settings (along with this
README). The file is a Lua 5.4 script: Term49 executes it
and then reads the resulting global variables (font_size,
metamode_keys, ...) to populate preferences. Any global
that is missing or has the wrong type falls back to the
compiled default, so a partial config is fine. If the
script fails to parse or run, Term49 keeps the previous
config (or compiled defaults at startup) and logs the
error.

SECURITY: .term49.lua runs as a full Lua program with the
standard library (including os and io). Treat it like a
shell rc -- only run a .term49.lua you wrote or trust.

Term49 never rewrites .term49.lua, so your comments and
formatting survive. To re-apply a saved file at runtime,
press metamode 'r' (the default reload_config binding).
Font-size changes from metamode i/o/p are session-only
and are not written back to the file.

Each of the preference options and their default is shown
below.

font_path = "/usr/fonts/font_repository/monotype/andalemo.ttf"
-- The path to the Unicode TTF font file to use in the
-- shell. This can be an absolute or relative path.

font_size = 16
-- The font size for the shell. Term49 will try to guess
-- a reasonable size for your screen, but otherwise
-- defaults to this value. Metamode i / o / p change this
-- at runtime (increase / decrease / reset).

text_color = { 255, 255, 255 }
-- The color for text as { r, g, b }. For classic green
-- text on black, use { 0, 255, 0 }.

background_color = { 0, 0, 0 }
-- The background color as { r, g, b }.

screen_idle_awake = false
-- If true, Term49 will force the screen to stay awake
-- when idle instead of dimming and eventually turning
-- off as usual.

auto_show_vkb = true
-- If true, automatically display the virtual keyboard at
-- launch time unless you have a Bluetooth keyboard. Set
-- to false to disable. On a Passport this is always true
-- so the symbol keys can be accessed.

metamode_doubletap_key = 61666
-- Which key to double tap in order to toggle metamode on
-- and off. This is an integer corresponding to the key
-- symbol and defaults to KEYCODE_RIGHT_SHIFT.

metamode_doubletap_delay = 500000000
-- When double tapping to invoke metamode, the time in
-- which to double tap, in nanoseconds. Defaults to 0.5
-- seconds.

keyhold_actions = true
-- Enables special actions when holding down a key. By
-- default Term49 will upcase letters if you hold them,
-- and will toggle metamode on if you hold space.

keyhold_actions_exempt = { 8, 13 }
-- These keycodes do not trigger special handling when
-- held down, so they will repeat as usual. Defaults to
-- { KEYCODE_BACKSPACE, KEYCODE_RETURN }.

metamode_hold_key = 32
-- If keyhold_actions is true, holding this key toggles
-- metamode on. Defaults to KEYCODE_SPACE.

keyhold_accents = true
-- If true, holding an accentable letter pops an accent
-- chooser (for example holding 'e' offers e/é/è/ê/ë).

tty_encoding = "UTF-8"
-- Term49 uses 16 bit Unicode internally, but when doing
-- IO with the tty / shell, will convert between Unicode
-- and the tty_encoding. Defaults to UTF-8, but can be
-- any encoding that the International Components for
-- Unicode (ICU) understands. Changes to tty_encoding
-- need a restart to take effect (reload_config does not
-- re-open the converter).

metamode_hitbox = { 0, 0, 100, 100 }
-- The area of the screen that you touch to toggle
-- metamode, as { x, y, w, h } in pixels measured from
-- the top left corner. Defaults to a 100x100 box in the
-- top left.

metamode_keys = {
  { "e", "\x1B" },
  { "t", "\t" },
}
-- When in metamode, pressing the given key will send the
-- specified string into the shell and then turn off
-- metamode. Lua string escapes apply: "\x1B" is ESC and
-- "\t" is TAB. You can use any character string here,
-- including UTF-8 sequences.
--
-- Term49 supports terminfo aliases for some common escape
-- sequences. If a metamode key's value is one of kcuu1,
-- kcud1, kcuf1, kcub1, khome, kend, or kf1 through kf12,
-- the correct escape sequence will be sent for cursor up,
-- down, right, left, home, end, or the corresponding
-- function key.

metamode_sticky_keys = {
  { "k", "kcuu1" },
  { "j", "kcud1" },
  { "l", "kcuf1" },
  { "h", "kcub1" },
}
-- Like metamode_keys above, but leaves metamode on
-- afterwards, so you can press another metamode key
-- immediately.

metamode_func_keys = {
  { "a", "alt_down" },
  { "c", "ctrl_down" },
  { "s", "rescreen" },
  { "v", "paste_clipboard" },
  { "i", "font_size_increase" },
  { "o", "font_size_decrease" },
  { "p", "font_size_reset" },
  { "r", "reload_config" },
  { "?", "help_overlay" },
}
-- These keys map to built-in functions in Term49:
--
--   alt_down            Hold the alt key, applied to the
--                       next keypress.
--   ctrl_down           Hold the ctrl key.
--   rescreen            Reinitialize the screen surface
--                       and fonts, including font size.
--   paste_clipboard     Paste the system clipboard. No
--                       character conversion is done.
--   font_size_increase  Bump font_size by one (session
--                       only; not written to disk).
--   font_size_decrease  Drop font_size by one.
--   font_size_reset     Restore font_size to the value
--                       in .term49.lua (or the compiled
--                       default).
--   reload_config       Re-run ~/.term49.lua and apply
--                       the new values live.
--   metamode_toggle     Toggle metamode (also bindable as
--                       a chord, e.g. shift+sym).
--   help_overlay        Show/hide the on-screen keybinding
--                       cheat sheet (any key dismisses it).
--
-- A value of the form "lua:<name>" calls the Lua
-- function `<name>` defined in .term49.lua. The function
-- runs under pcall, so a Lua error logs but does not
-- crash Term49. Two helpers are exposed in the `term`
-- table:
--
--   term.font_size_get()        current font_size
--   term.font_size_set(n)       set font_size (session)
--   term.action("<builtin>")    invoke any builtin name
--                               from the list above
--
-- Example: to bind metamode 'z' to a custom zoom-in:
--   function zoom_in()
--     term.font_size_set(term.font_size_get() + 3)
--   end
-- and add  { "z", "lua:zoom_in" }  above.

chord_bindings = {
  { key = "alt", mods = {"shift"}, action = "ctrl_down",       label = "shift+alt = Ctrl" },
  { key = "sym", mods = {"shift"}, action = "metamode_toggle", label = "shift+sym = Meta" },
}
-- Modifier-aware chords: a trigger key plus a set of held
-- or stuck modifiers dispatch to an action. Distinct from
-- metamode (a mode you toggle) -- a chord fires directly.
--
-- Each record has:
--   key     the trigger. A single character ("t", "1") is
--           that key; named keys are "sym", "alt", "shift",
--           "space", "enter", "tab", "escape", "backspace".
--   mods    a list of "ctrl", "alt", "shift", "sym". The
--           match is exact (ctrl+t does not fire on
--           ctrl+shift+t). An empty/absent mods = no
--           modifiers. Order in the list is irrelevant.
--   action  any value metamode_func_keys accepts: raw
--           bytes, a terminfo name, a builtin, or
--           "lua:<fn>". Two builtins are chord-oriented:
--             metamode_toggle  toggle metamode
--             help_overlay     toggle the on-screen list
--                              of chord bindings (uses the
--                              label fields)
--   label   optional text shown in the help overlay.
--
-- The bare BlackBerry keyboard has no Ctrl key (only
-- shift/alt/sym, all sticky tap-to-stick), so the two
-- defaults above reclaim two otherwise-unused modifier
-- combinations: tap shift then alt to arm Ctrl for the
-- next key; tap shift then sym to toggle metamode. Because
-- the modifiers are sticky, either order works.
--
-- ctrl/alt-prefixed chords are aimed at an external
-- keyboard (which has a real Ctrl); on the built-in
-- keyboard use the shift+alt / shift+sym combinations or
-- metamode. Set chord_bindings = {} to disable the
-- defaults. A chord on a modifier key only fires when an
-- extra modifier is already stuck, so plain shift/alt/sym
-- keep their normal text-entry behavior.
--
-- show_help_on_startup (a boolean, with the other settings
-- near the top of this file) pops the help overlay once on
-- launch, like a hotkey cheat sheet.

rescreen_for_symmenu = true
-- If true, opening a symbol menu causes the screen to
-- readjust to the new visible dimensions.

main_symmenu = {
  { { "a", "=" }, { "s", "-" }, { "d", "*" },
    { "f", "/" }, { "g", "\\" }, { "h", "|" },
    { "j", "&" }, { "k", "'" }, { "l", "\"" } },
  { { "q", "~" }, { "w", "`" }, { "e", "{" },
    { "r", "}" }, { "t", "[" }, { "y", "]" },
    { "u", "<" }, { "i", ">" }, { "o", "^" },
    { "p", "%" } },
}
-- For devices with a Sym key, this controls the contents
-- of the symbol menu.
--
-- main_symmenu is a list of rows; each row is a list of
-- { key, symbol } pairs. Keys are keyboard shortcuts,
-- symbols can be any string (including UTF-8). The menu
-- is drawn from the bottom of the screen upward, so the
-- first row is drawn at the bottom. The menu is scaled
-- to fit the widest row across the screen width. There
-- is no limit on the number of items, but all shortcut
-- keys must be unique.

sticky_sym_key = false
-- For devices with a Sym key, controls whether the Sym
-- menu stays open after hitting a key.

sticky_shift_key = true
-- For devices with physical shift keys, this setting
-- controls whether the shift key 'sticks' after being
-- pressed, and applies to the next keypress.

sticky_alt_key = true
-- Same idea as sticky_shift_key, for the alt key.

allow_resize_columns = false
-- This setting controls whether a program can change the
-- number of columns on the screen. Defaults to false (no
-- resizing). When set to true, programs can set the
-- number of columns (80 or 132), but this may make the
-- text really small. Use the metamode 'rescreen'
-- function to reset the font size to your preference.

prefs_version = 4
-- The schema version of .term49.lua, written by Term49
-- on first run. You don't need to set this by hand.

===========================================================

3. WHAT'S INSTALLED

The default userland that comes with your device is pretty
sparse, and some of the versions are somewhat old. You have
the basic file utilities (ls, less, mv, cp, etc.), but no
compiler and no man pages. Some commands have 'use'
information available (eg. 'use /bin/ls') which can help
out with the flags for the QNX userland tools. You do have
python so you can get some work done with that. You can edit
files with elvis, which is like vi.

The default version of ksh is quite old, so Term49 bundles
mksh, which is better in general and much better about
unicode in particular. Term49 also bundles ssh, scp, sftp
and ssh-keygen, due to popular demand.

It is possible to install other programs and round out the
userland, as described below in FOR DEVELOPERS.

===========================================================

4. FOR DEVELOPERS

Term49 is Open Source

Term49 is a 'from scratch' terminal emulator in that it is
not a port of another program. It uses SDL to draw to the
screen, freetype to render fonts, Lua for configuration
and scripting, ICU to convert between character encodings,
and libghostty-vt as its terminal parser/state model. It
is not by any means perfect, and will certainly have bugs
or missing features. Because the author enjoyed access to
so many high quality libraries that made the development
of Term49 significantly easier, Term49 is open source:

https://github.com/mordak/Term49

If you find a bug, missing feature, or something else, feel
free to bring it up on github. Pull requests are welcome.

Adding Programs to the Userland

Because you have python and sh, and BlackBerry provides a
cross compiler with their dev tools, it is possible to
cross compile programs and install them into your userland.
Because the dev tools use gcc, it is possible to cross
compile gcc and install that into your userland, and then
you can compile other programs locally. This is not
trivial, but is also not too hard. The author has a very
rudimentary bootstraap system that will cross compile gcc
and some other useful tools and then install them over the
air onto your device - no root required.  You can give it a
try if you like:

https://github.com/mordak/playbook-dev-tools

This has been tested with a Linux (ubuntu) build machine
and a Passport target. Again, feel free to use github to
contribute or raise issues. In the long term the goal is to
port some kind of existing package management system and
bundle it with Term49, but we're not there yet.

Happy hacking!

